BCC_Q({"lv": "910", "y": 2020, "n": 13, "part": "C", "topic": "opt", "title": "DNA Sequence", "answer": 3, "story": "<p>Genes in cells contain DNA which can tell us a lot about a living\nthing. A DNA sequence is formed from nitrogen bases. Each nitrogen base\nis one of four types: Adenine (A), Guanine (G), Cytosine (C), or Thymine\n(T). DNA can mutate to form a new sequence that is different from the\noriginal sequence.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:50%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>Vormi is a creature for which each mutation is one of three\nkinds:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Substitution: Change one occurrence of a base to another base\ntype.<\/li><\/ol><\/p> <p>\nExample: AGGTC becomes AGATC (change second G to A).\n<li>Deletion: Remove one occurrence of a base.<\/li><\/p> <p>\nExample: AGGTC becomes AGTC (delete one G).\n<li>Duplication: Replace one occurrence of a base with two\noccurrences of the same base.<\/li><\/p> <p>\nExample: AGGTC becomes AGGTTC (duplicate T).", "question": "<p>If Vormi’s DNA sequence is initially GTATCG, what sequence\n<span><strong>cannot<\/strong><\/span> be the result after exactly three\nmutations?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li>GCAATG<\/li>\n<li>ATTATCCG<\/li>\n<li>GAATGC<\/li>\n<li>GGTAAAC<\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(D) GGTAAAC<\/p>", "explanation": "<p>Options A, B, and C can be obtained in exactly three steps:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Option A: substitution (T to C <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> GCATCG), substitution (T to A\n<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> GCAACG), substitution\n(C to T <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span>\nGCAATG).<\/li>\n<li>Option B: substitution (G to A <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> ATATCG), duplication (T <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> ATTATCG), duplication (C\n<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> ATTATCCG).<\/li>\n<li>Option C: substitution (T to A <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> GAATCG), substitution (C to G\n<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> GAATGG), substitution\n(G to C <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span>\nGAATGC).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Options A, B, and C can be reached by 3 steps of gene mutation, while\nOption D can be reached by 4 steps: duplication (G <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> GGTATCG), duplication (A\n<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> GGTAATCG), substitution\n(T to A <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span> GGTAAACG),\ndeletion (G <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rightarrow\\)<\/span>\nGGTAAAC).<\/p> <p>\nTo see why Option D cannot be reached in exactly 3 steps, notice that\nthere must be more duplications than deletions because there is one more\nbase in GGTAAAC than in GTATCG. Thus, if there are exactly 3 steps\nresulting in Option D, there are only two possibilities:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>There are 2 duplications and 1 deletion. In this case, the only\nway to generate AAA in the middle of the sequence is to duplicate A\ntwice. The single deletion cannot be used to both match the GG at the\nbeginning of GGTAAAC and the C at the end of GGTAAAC.<\/li>\n<li>There is 1 duplication and 2 substitutions. In this case, the\nonly way to generate AAA in the middle of the sequence is to duplicate A\nand perform a substitution on either T. This leaves a single\nsubstitution which cannot be used to both match the GG at the beginning\nof GGTAAAC and the C at the end of GGTAAAC.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Since neither of these possibilities can be realized, we know Option\nD cannot be reached in exactly 3 steps.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>To solve this problem, we need to find the distance between one DNA\nsequence and another. We have to try all the choices and calculate the\nminimum distance of each given DNA sequence. We can think of each DNA\nsequence as a <span><em>word<\/em><\/span>, over an alphabet composed of\nthe letters {A, C, G, T}.<\/p> <p>\nThe minimum distance between two words is known as the\n<span><em>Levenshtein distance<\/em><\/span>. The Levenshtein distance is\nsometimes referred to as the <span><em>edit distance<\/em><\/span> because\nwe wish to find the minimum number of single character edits to\ntransform one word into the other. Typically, operations allowed for an\nedit are <span><em>insertion<\/em><\/span> (adding one character),\n<span><em>deletion<\/em><\/span> (removing one character), and\n<span><em>substitution<\/em><\/span> (replacing one character with\nanother). In this task, the insertion operation is replaced by the\nduplication operation.<\/p> <p>\nOne important application of computing the Levenshtein distance is a\n<span><em>spell checker<\/em><\/span>, since we want to find the “closest”\nwords to a misspelled word.<\/p>"});